---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { LinkPreview } from "astro-embed";
import { Tweet } from "astro-embed";
---

<Layout>
  <main class="flex flex-col w-full justify-center items-center">
    <div class="w-1/2 flex flex-col gap-10 my-[10vh]">
      <div class="header flex flex-col gap-5">
        <h1 class="text-4xl">Sources</h1>
        <p>
          External sources that inspired and informed the content of this
          website, including other advocacy websites, news articles, and social
          media posts.
        </p>
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Websites</h2>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-25 w-full">
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Battle for the Net</h2>
          <LinkPreview id="https://www.battleforthenet.com/" />
          <p>
            This is a high-impact grassroots mobilization hub run by the
            advocacy groups Fight for the Future and Demand Progress. It serves
            as a "digital headquarters" for internet users to take direct
            political action. By making advocacy accessible to everyone with a
            phone or computer, it ensures that voices from underrepresented
            communities—those most affected by the digital divide—are heard by
            lawmakers in Washington.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Electronic Frontier Foundation (EFF)</h2>
          <LinkPreview id="https://www.eff.org/issues/net-neutrality" />
          <p>
            The EFF is the leading nonprofit defending civil liberties in the
            digital world. Their Net Neutrality page provides deep legal and
            technical analysis of why the "open pipe" model is essential for a
            free society. They provide the "intellectual ammunition" for the
            movement, filing legal amicus briefs in court cases and explaining
            how technical ISP practices (like packet sniffing) violate user
            rights.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Public Knowledge Net Neutrality</h2>
          <LinkPreview id="https://publicknowledge.org/issue/net-neutrality/" />
          <p>
            Public Knowledge is a Washington D.C.-based public interest group
            that works at the intersection of copyright, telecommunications, and
            internet law. They focus on "Universal Service," advocating for
            policies that treat the internet like a public utility. This is a
            key solution to the digital divide, as it would force ISPs to
            provide affordable, high-quality access to everyone, regardless of
            their zip code.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Measurement Lab</h2>
          <LinkPreview id="https://www.measurementlab.net/" />
          <p>
            M-Lab is an open-source project and the world's largest collection
            of open internet performance data, co-founded by researchers and
            Google. They provide the scientific evidence for advocacy. If an ISP
            claims they aren't throttling data, M-Lab’s global database can be
            used by researchers to prove whether or not certain types of traffic
            are being slowed down.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Free Press Net Neutrality</h2>
          <LinkPreview
            id="https://www.freepress.net/issues/free-open-internet/net-neutrality"
          />
          <p>
            Free Press is a national, nonpartisan organization that advocates
            for media and technology policies that promote a free and open
            internet. Their Net Neutrality page provides resources and updates
            on the ongoing fight to preserve an open internet, highlighting the
            impact of policy decisions on consumers and communities.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <hr />
      <h2 class="text-2xl">Social Media</h2>
      <div class="flex flex-col gap-25 w-full">
        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">@FightForTheFuture - X</h2>
          <Tweet id="https://x.com/fightfortheftr/status/972970426291900416" />
          <p>
            This user on X(formerly Twitter) advocates for digital rights and
            net neutrality. And not only that, they actively engage their
            followers in discussions and campaigns to protect these rights.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">Free Press - Youtube</h2>
          <p>
            This channel on YouTube provides videos and updates on digital
            rights and net neutrality, helping to educate the public and
            mobilize support for an open internet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">@EFForg - Instagram</h2>
          <p>
            This Instagram account shares updates and information on digital
            rights and net neutrality, engaging followers with visual content
            and campaigns to raise awareness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">r/NetNeutrality - Reddit</h2>
          <p>
            This subreddit is dedicated to discussions and news about net
            neutrality, providing a platform for users to share information, ask
            questions, and engage in conversations about digital rights and
            internet freedom.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h2 class="text-2xl">@eff.org - Bluesky</h2>
          <p>
            This Bluesky account shares updates and information on digital
            rights and net neutrality, engaging followers with content and
            campaigns to raise awareness.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
